  "authoritative_metadata": "manifest.json",
  "title": "What screening gives the brace dictionary",
  "status": "Proved",
  "claim": "What the screening lemmas give the brace dictionary, in Fano trees (spaces built from Fano atoms alone). (1) Contraction theorem: if one line creates at another in the chain of their atoms (the closures of the atoms on the tree path between them, glued at its cuts), it does so in the whole space; the converse holds when every atom off the path emits toward it. (2) Block lemma: every line of a Fano atom creates at every other line of it. (3) The touching rule: in every gluing of two atoms (294) and of three atoms in a chain (576), and on four-atom paths, a removal in one atom creates at a line of another exactly when that line touches its atom's cut toward the first (it lies on the receiving cut, or its atom emits there), whatever the removed line's role; this is BRACE-TREE's dictionary in one rule. So in every Fano tree a removal creates at every line of another atom at most two apart that touches the cut toward it. Non-creation at lines that do not touch holds in every chain and in four 25-point trees, and in general follows from ticket A's pendant-line statement; distance three is computed. The source rule stays computed, now resting on that statement and on a transfer along chains.",
